-- js-pack-home.rofl — WHERE A `node_kind` ROW BELONGS, AS A RULE RATHER THAN
-- AS A CONVENTION WRITTEN OUT IN THREE COMMENTS.
--
-- `w_vocabulary_home` asked whether the `node_kind` rows scattered over seven
-- fact packs should be gathered into `facts/js-kinds.rofl`, the pack whose NAME
-- promises them. Measured 2026-09-09 before moving anything:
--
--     100 js kinds, 274 rows, 7 packs.  facts/js-kinds.rofl declares 32.
--     35 kinds declared once, 11 declared five times.
--
-- The minority is real and it is not what the file name suggests, because that
-- file's own first line says what it is: *a DELIBERATELY TINY sample, enough to
-- test the mechanism and not one node more*. It is the fixture
-- `test/js-model.test.ts` pins number for number, plus the twenty-three kinds
-- declared on 2026-09-08 to make the denominator the LANGUAGE. It was never the
-- vocabulary's home; it is the BASE every world in the suite loads.
--
-- THE RULE THE TREE ALREADY OBEYS, stated here for the first time:
--
--     A `node_kind(js, K)` row belongs in the pack of layer P iff
--       (1) P SPEAKS ABOUT K — some fact P asserts carries K in a position that
--           holds kinds: a verdict in one of the six ledgers, or a row in one of
--           the twenty-six kind tables the rules assert beside themselves — or
--       (2) P is the BASE pack, which every world loads and which therefore
--           declares what a layer pack may borrow.
--
-- Clause (1) is not a new convention. It is the two audits that already exist,
-- read backwards. `orphan_claim[audit]` refuses a claim whose kind the world
-- does not know — that is what put `boolean_literal` and thirty more into the
-- layer packs on 2026-09-05. `kind_undeclared[audit]` and
-- `rule_opinion_unlisted[audit]` refuse an opinion held in the rules and absent
-- from the vocabulary — that is what put `tsas_expression` into
-- facts/js-callgraph.rofl, which carries no verdict about it at all and whose
-- rules read it as a callee shape and a cast.
--
-- MEASURED OVER ALL SEVEN PACKS, honest tree, 2026-09-09: `kind_home_unbacked`
-- is EMPTY, and 24 rows fire the moment the base clause is removed. **The rows
-- are already where the rule says they belong.** What was missing is that
-- nothing said so, and that is what this file is.
--
-- AND THE COPIES ARE THE PRICE OF PACKS THAT LOAD INDEPENDENTLY, which is the
-- half of the decision the item's statement does not mention. Gathering the
-- rows into facts/js-kinds.rofl would not tidy the tree, it would ENLARGE every
-- partial world in the suite — the world `test/js-model.test.ts` builds from
-- that pack alone goes from 32 declared kinds to 100 — and it would put 68 rows
-- in a pack that speaks about none of them, which is exactly what clause (1)
-- forbids. `test/js-pack-home.test.ts` measures that counterfactual world by
-- world instead of arguing about it.

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHICH PACK A ROW CAME FROM. There is no file identity in this kernel — the
-- finest grain the store records is the PRINCIPAL a load named, which boot.rofl
-- says where `loader/3` is defined — so a loader that wants this audit gives
-- each pack a `who` of its own. `test/js-pack-home.test.ts` is that loader.
--
-- A LAYER IS TWO FILES AND ONE PRINCIPAL. `facts/js-callgraph.rofl` and
-- `rules/js-callgraph.rofl` load under the same `who`, because the question
-- this file asks is which LAYER should carry a kind and a layer is both of its
-- files. The price is stated rather than hidden: a row in the wrong FILE of the
-- right layer is invisible here. The alternative — one principal per file —
-- was tried and measured worse: it needs a hand-written table pairing each
-- fact pack with its rule pack, and a hand table of packs is the thing
-- `fkind_slot` below exists to avoid.
--
-- THE OTHER LIMIT IS THE HOST'S DISCIPLINE ABOUT `who`, exactly as it is for
-- `widened[audit]`: packs concatenated into a single `load()` — which is what
-- most test files in this suite do — are one pack here, and this audit says
-- nothing at all about them. That is why it lives in its own pack with its own
-- loader rather than being switched on for every world.
pack_fact(P, Rel, Args) :- asserted_by($fact(Rel, main, Args), P, _).

-- THE ARGUMENT WALK, one position at a time. Same shape as the one
-- rules/js-vocabulary.rofl runs over `premise_lit`, and for the same reason:
-- the reified argument list is a cons chain and Datalog cannot index into one.
-- That file walks what the RULES MATCH; this one walks what the FACTS SAY, and
-- between them the two cover both halves of "the model has an opinion about K".
fwalk(P, Rel, 1, Args) :- pack_fact(P, Rel, Args).
fwalk(P, Rel, J, T)    :- fwalk(P, Rel, I, $cons(_, T)), J is I + 1.
fslot(P, Rel, I, A)    :- fwalk(P, Rel, I, $cons(A, _)).

-- WHICH POSITIONS CARRY A KIND — DISCOVERED, NOT DECLARED, which is the
-- load-bearing choice and it is taken deliberately from
-- rules/js-vocabulary.rofl. A hand table of ledgers rots: the day somebody adds
-- a seventh relation whose second argument is a kind, a list nobody updated
-- reports zero and means nothing. A position IS a kind position when a DECLARED
-- kind stands in it, so the audit configures itself from the facts it audits.
--
-- MEASURED 2026-09-09 over the seven fact packs and six rule packs: THIRTY-TWO
-- positions qualify — the six verdict ledgers (`handled`, `ignored`,
-- `unknown_because`, `checked`, `shape_of`, `kind_absent_ok`) plus twenty-six
-- kind TABLES the rule packs assert beside themselves: `abrupt_kind`,
-- `callee_shape`, `value_transparent`, `obj_kind_class`, `block_scope_kind`,
-- `completion_kind` and the rest. A hand list would have had the six and missed
-- the twenty-six, and the twenty-six are exactly what makes `tsas_expression`
-- legitimate where it sits.
--
-- `node_kind` ITSELF IS EXCLUDED and the exclusion is the whole audit: without
-- it every declaration would back itself and the rule would say nothing. Same
-- exemption `test/js-vocabulary.test.ts` writes as KIND_TABLE_EXEMPT, arrived
-- at from the other end — there because the table is the declaration rather
-- than a consumer of it, here because a row must be earned by something OTHER
-- than its own existence.
fkind_slot(Rel, I) :- fslot(_, Rel, I, A), node_kind(js, A).

pack_declares(P, K) :- fslot(P, node_kind, 2, K), node_kind(js, K).
pack_speaks(P, K)   :- fslot(P, Rel, I, K), fkind_slot(Rel, I), Rel != node_kind.

-- THE BASE PACK, one row and not a waiver list. It names the pack every world
-- loads, whose job is to declare what a layer may borrow; it names no kind, so
-- it cannot go quiet on a row somebody parks there. The difference is the
-- point: a per-kind exemption table would have absorbed all 68 rows a wholesale
-- gather creates, and this row absorbs none of them.
edb(base_pack).
base_pack(p_js_kinds).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE VERDICT. A row in a pack that does not speak about the kind opens cells
-- nobody in that world can answer. That is the defect in BOTH directions of the
-- decision `w_vocabulary_home` records — what a wholesale gather into
-- facts/js-kinds.rofl would do 68 times over, and what a sweep dropping a row
-- in the nearest open file does once.
--
-- WHERE THIS CHECK IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, three answers and none of
-- them the obvious one:
--   * it cannot tell facts/js-X.rofl from rules/js-X.rofl — see the principal
--     note above;
--   * it cannot see a kind a layer's rules only MATCH on. `premise_lit` has no
--     file and never will, so a kind backed by a premise and by no asserted row
--     would be a FALSE POSITIVE here. Measured 2026-09-09: there are none, in
--     any of the six layer packs — every kind a layer declares stands in some
--     row that layer asserts. Recorded as `f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does`.
--   * it says nothing about a world it is not loaded into, which is every world
--     in the suite but one. It is a check on the PACKS, and `orphan_claim` stays
--     the check on the worlds.
kind_home_unbacked[audit](P, K) :- pack_declares(P, K), not pack_speaks(P, K),
                                   not base_pack(P).

-- THE OTHER DIRECTION IS A REPORT, NOT A GATE, and saying which is which is
-- most of what this file decides. A pack that speaks about a kind it does not
-- declare is BORROWING from a pack it is always loaded beside; there are 118 of
-- those on the honest tree and every one is legitimate, because
-- `orphan_claim[audit]` is what refuses the illegitimate case per WORLD.
-- Written as a gate this would be red on an honest checkout, and CLAUDE.md
-- already says what happens to a gate in that state.
pack_borrows[audit](P, K) :- pack_speaks(P, K), not pack_declares(P, K),
                             node_kind(js, K).

-- A CHECK MEASURED AND NOT BUILT, with the measurement as the reason. The
-- tempting third rule is *a kind the base pack declares that some layer now
-- speaks about should have moved to that layer* — `residue_stale`. It was
-- written, run, and deleted: it reports 24 rows on an honest tree and every one
-- is a legitimate BORROW from the base, because a layer pack is allowed to
-- judge a kind the base declares and `orphan_claim` is content. The criterion
-- that would separate stale from borrowed is *no world loads this layer without
-- the base*, which is a fact about the LOADERS and not about the packs, and
-- this file cannot see a loader. So the rule would have been a gate red on an
-- honest checkout — the state CLAUDE.md says ends with the gate switched off.
